<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Banate of Severin</h1><p class="st-meta">1228 CE – 1524 CE (approx.) · capital Drobeta-Turnu Severin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hungarian frontier province at the Iron Gates of the Danube, a defensive march against Bulgarians, Cumans and later the Ottomans.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II's</a> son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iv-of-hungary">Béla</a> set up the banate around 1228–1233, in lands taken from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-gates">Iron Gates</a>, around the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drobeta-turnu-severin">Severin</a> (Drobeta-Turnu Severin) built on Roman ruins. In 1247 Béla IV granted it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>, though they soon left.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A royal ban commanded the castle and the border, collecting tribute from the Romanian voivodes and knezes beyond, such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/voivodeship-of-litovoi">Voivodeship of Litovoi</a>. Control shifted repeatedly with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia</a>, whose rulers took the title ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drobeta-turnu-severin">Severin</a>. In the 15th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> held it against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a> was ban.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ottoman raids steadily wore it down. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drobeta-turnu-severin">Severin</a> fortress fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1524, two years before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Mohács</a>, and the banate ceased to exist. The region passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wallachia">Wallachia</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oltenia">Oltenia's</a> western edge.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/banate-of-severin.jpg" alt="Banate of Severin" width="320" height="293"><figcaption>Image: WikiEditor2004 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banate_of_severin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1228 CE – 1524 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Drobeta-Turnu Severin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John Hunyadi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity; Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hungarian; Romanian; Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>, <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Banat of Szörény, Severin Banate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1376&amp;focus=banate-of-severin" aria-label="Banate of Severin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5221 600 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Banate of Severin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5541 856L5446 783M5541 856L5446 783M5541 856L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5446 783h0M5446 783h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5541 856h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5541 856h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Drobeta-Turnu Severin. <a href="/atlas?year=1376&amp;focus=banate-of-severin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

